diagnosis_text:
The reported symptom — PC4 (172.16.10.4) being unable to reach Laptop0 (172.16.20.10) — cannot be reproduced in the live lab. The network is operating correctly in every measurable respect.

PC4 sits on VLAN 10 (switch1 br_vlan10, port eth1) and Laptop0 on VLAN 20 (switch1 br_vlan20, port eth7). Inter-VLAN routing is performed by router0, which terminates both VLANs as 802.1Q subinterfaces on its eth0 (eth0.10 = 172.16.10.1/24, eth0.20 = 172.16.20.1/24) via a trunk through mswitch1. Active pings in both directions complete with 0% packet loss and TTL=63 (exactly one routing hop through router0), confirming the full forwarding chain is intact. Same-VLAN reachability to PC5, Laptop2, and Laptop3 also succeeds at TTL=64.

No packet filters exist on any device in the path: iptables FORWARD and ebtables FORWARD chains are empty with ACCEPT policy on switch1, mswitch1, router0, and laptop0. FRR on router0 carries no access-lists or distribute-lists. Router0's RIP table shows both 172.16.10.0/24 and 172.16.20.0/24 as directly connected and stable. Interface error counters on all data-plane links are zero.

Root cause: there is no fault. The user's ticket describes a connectivity problem that does not exist in the current lab state; the inter-VLAN path between PC4 and Laptop0 is fully functional.

